# Unified terminal (V10): one `marin` for chat and read-only code, 2026-10-20

Machine and models as in 11-coding-v0.log (Ryzen 7 260, RTX 5050 Laptop 40 W, Qwen3.5-4B Q8 CUDA INT8). Goal: make
`marin` the single entry point without a separate `marin serve`, keep every documented script and flag working, and
leave the kernels, quantization, scheduler and caches alone.

## Design (smallest thing that worked)

- Interface: the existing terminal app (`term/`) gained a mode. The palette (`/` then type to search) is the slash
  command surface: `Mode: chat`, `Mode: code`, `Switch model…`, `Status`, `Settings`, `Clear conversation`, `Help`,
  `Exit` (the others stay). Typing `/mode code`, `/model`, `/status`, `/settings`, `/clear`, `/help`, `/exit` selects
  the matching action; a piped line starting with one of those words is the same command, anything else is text.
- Runtime: no server child process. Code mode runs the same tool loop as `marin code`, answered by the model the
  terminal already loaded (`code::local::LocalBrain` behind a `Brain` trait that the HTTP client also implements). So
  there is no port, no stale process to clean up and no second copy of the weights.
- `dialog::Dialog` owns the two conversations (chat history in `Chat`, code history in `CodeChat`); a mode switch
  keeps both and says so; `/clear` forgets the current one; a new model resets both and says so.
- `setup.rs` (flags, saved config, precision) and `choose.rs` (which model) moved out of `bin/marin.rs`.
- `code::ui` renders replies with the app's own `ReplyWriter` (`marin >` prefix, markdown, same colors), so chat and
  code answers look alike; the stand-alone client prompt is now the app's `you >` too.

## Semantics

Launch commands: `marin` (chat), `marin code [PATH]` (code), `marin run [TEXT]` (TEXT or a pipe: one answer; nothing on a
terminal: the chat), `marin serve` (headless API, unchanged), `marin plan|model|show` (unchanged).

Precedence for backend, `temp`, `max_tokens`: explicit flag (before or after the subcommand) > saved config > built-in
default. Saved: `model`, `backend` (changed in `/model` / Switch backend), `temp`, `max_tokens` (changed in the UI),
and for a packed 8-bit model `quantize` + `quant_group` tied to that model. Flags are never written; `--model X` still
becomes the default model as documented. `marin serve` ignores the saved backend and sampling values, so a server
started by an old script behaves as before. A missing, unreadable or invalid config is reported once ("ignoring the
saved settings") and the next save replaces it; fields it lacks (an old `{"model": …}` file) simply stay unset.

Model choice: `--model` > saved model (if still a model folder, else a note and the steps below) > the only chat model
found > on a terminal a list ranked by the planner (fits the GPU first, larger before smaller; each row says "fits the
GPU", "runs on the CPU", "will not fit: too big for this machine's memory (try --quantize int8)", and "packed 8-bit") >
with no models a terminal asks for a folder (three tries, `~/` expanded, base models and non-models refused with the
reason) and a pipe gets "no chat model found" with the download hint. Several models on a pipe: "several models found: a,
b" with `--model`.

Packed 8-bit models (decision changed during the milestone): an early version loaded them as INT8 whenever the folder
said so. That broke the documented rule and an existing test (`both_clis_require_explicit_int8_and_use_the_exported_bundle`:
"BF16 default cannot silently change precision"), so it was removed. Now: explicit `--model packed` without
`--quantize int8` still fails, with a hint naming the flag and the model list; picking the model from the list is the
opt-in and is saved with the model; a user who already had a packed model saved (no opt-in recorded) is asked once on a
terminal ("… can only be loaded that way. Load it? [Y/n]") and never again.

Server lifecycle: `marin`/`/mode code` start nothing. `marin code` without `--port` probes `127.0.0.1:8000` (300 ms
connect, 1 s read) and, if a Marin server answers and neither `--model` nor `--backend` was given, uses it and says so
(a server cannot honor a model or backend choice); otherwise it loads its own model. `--port N` always means the server
on N. `marin serve --port P` on a busy port now fails before loading the model with "port P is already in use" and a hint
(it used to load first and then print a bare OS error).

Hints added: out-of-memory / no fit → "try --quantize int8 (half the memory), a smaller --ctx, or close other
programs"; the packed-artifact load error → the flag or the list. Code mode refuses a window under 2048 tokens with the
`--ctx` advice and stays in chat. `marin code` starts at an 8192 default window (reduced with a note if memory is short);
chat keeps 4096.

## In-process code mode: a replay problem, found by measuring

First working version, same question as the server run (`Where is the constant SLICE defined…?` then a follow-up):
rounds took 8.8, 11.6, 12.8 and 21.1 s for prompts of 914, 1366, 1414 and 2465 tokens, and Ctrl-C answered only after
2.3-3.3 s (once it was lost for 12 s). Cause: on Qwen3.5 GPU, shortening the cache to the prefix a new prompt shares
(the reply's own start differs from how history renders it) forces a replay of everything cached; the server avoids it
with a state checkpoint at the end of the history. Fix, using the same existing API and no engine change: read the
history part of the prompt in 64-token slices (`Session::prefill`, cancellable between slices), take
`Engine::session_checkpoint` there, generate the reply, and return the cache to the checkpoint
(`Session::return_to_boundary`), so the next prompt only reads what came after it. Where the engine cannot hold a second
copy (CPU, low VRAM) it behaves as before.

| | Before | After |
|---|---|---|
| Two-tool question, whole | 25.3 s | 14.6 s |
| Requests of that question | 4.4 / 8.8 / 11.6 s | 4.4 / 5.3 / 4.7 s |
| Follow-up question requests | 12.8 / 21.1 s | 2.3 / 10.1 s (the 10 s is 1,051 new tokens at 7.5 ms) |
| Ctrl-C to "cancelled" at 1.5 / 4 / 6 / 8 / 11 s | up to 3.3 s, once lost | 0.43 / 0.01 / 0.15 / 0.16 / 0.29 s |

## Tests added

- `tests/launch.rs` (12, tiny chat model, real binary): no model on a pipe; the only model remembered; several models
  refused on a pipe and `--model` accepted; a saved model gone; a damaged config; an old model-only config; flags vs saved
  vs default settings; `/help /model /mode /mode code /mode chat /clear` and an unknown `/word` as text on a pipe;
  `marin run` one-shot and piped; `marin code` with no server and no model; a port that answers with garbage or hangs up;
  `serve` on a busy port.
- `tests/modes_pty.rs` (5, `script` pseudo-terminal): `/mode chat|code` and `/status` from the palette, the window error
  that keeps chat going, help / clear / settings, `marin run` with no text opening the chat, Ctrl-C clear-then-exit with the
  terminal restored, and the upgrade question asked once and not again.
- Unit: `setup` (explicit-flag detection before/after the subcommand, saved values fill gaps and flags win, the packed
  opt-in follows its model and never overrides a flag), `config` (old file loads, update keeps other fields, damaged file
  reported and replaced), `actions` (each slash command finds its action).

## Manual checks on the real model (all passed)

| Case | Result |
|---|---|
| Fresh home, `~/models`, terminal | list ranked: Qwen3.5-4B-Q8 (packed 8-bit · fits the GPU · 4.35 GB), Qwen3-1.7B, Qwen3-0.6B, Qwen3.5-4B ("too big … try --quantize int8"); Enter loads it, config saved with the opt-in |
| Fresh home, no models, terminal | asks for a folder; `/tmp/not-a-model` and a base model refused with reasons; `~/models/Qwen3-0.6B` accepted, loaded, saved |
| Saved packed model without opt-in (upgrade) | one [Y/n] question, saved, no question next launch |
| `marin --model <packed>` on a pipe | error + hint, exit 1 |
| `/mode code`, a question, `/status`, `/mode chat`, `/exit` in the palette (pseudo-terminal) | `›` tool lines, `marin >` answer, footer, status shows mode + memory, exit status 0 |
| Same on a pipe | same answers, 31.8 s for code + chat questions including load |
| Server on 8000 running, `marin code` | "using the Marin server on port 8000 (Qwen3.5-4B-Q8)", answered through it |
| Same with `--model Qwen3-0.6B --backend cpu` | loads its own, ignores the server |
| Server holding the GPU, `marin` chat on a pipe | planner falls back to the CPU (status shows `cpu`); on a terminal the planner's note is printed (code path, not captured) |
| CUDA hidden (`CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=-1`) | auto: CPU, answers; `--backend cuda`: "CUDA is not available" + hint |
| BF16 Qwen3.5-4B | "GPU has 7209 MB free but this needs 9297 MB; … CPU also cannot run" + the int8 hint |
| Ctrl-C while the model loads (pipe and terminal) | exit 130 within the second, terminal cooked |
| Ctrl-D at the prompt | process gone in 0.16 s |
| Server killed 3 s into a remote-client answer | "the connection broke", hint, exit 1 (from 11-coding-v0.log, unchanged) |

## Not done / still open

- Chat mode in the terminal still replays the conversation on Qwen3.5 GPU each turn (existing behavior; the checkpoint fix
  was applied to code mode only).
- Code mode's first question after a long chat reads its whole prompt (7.5 ms/token); the server's shared-prefix cache does
  not apply (different system prompt).
- Cancellation is heard within a slice (about 0.5 s) while the prompt is read, and per token while generating; a tool that
  is running finishes its current file first.
- The auto-reuse of a server on port 8000 is tested by hand only (the port is fixed).

## Verification

fmt, check, Clippy -D warnings, line-limit, unsafe clean. Workspace tests with MARIN_REQUIRE_CUDA=1 (real-model tests on)
and with MARIN_MATMUL_SCALAR=1: 723 passed, 0 failed, 5 ignored each (698 before this milestone). Pinned HF verify of
Qwen3.5-4B Q8 CUDA unchanged: max logit 1.31e-5, max layer 1.18e-5, normalized max 3.879e-6, relative RMS 3.288e-6.
One pseudo-terminal test raced the "keys typed during a reply are dropped" rule on a loaded machine (it typed `/` as the
reply ended); it now waits for the answer to end and the prompt to be back. A model switch no longer saves a backend that
only a flag chose, and clears a packed-model opt-in when the new model is not packed (both seen in a live switch).
No kernel, quantization, scheduler or cache code changed. Server-side: `context_window` and `usage` (earlier) and the
busy-port check in `marin serve`.
